[DRAFT] Answer:
```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>
#include <tuple>

/**
Check if the provided email address is valid. For example, abc.123@def.ghi.com.uk is a valid email address. Cover as many cases as possible to avoid false negatives.

@param email The email address to check
@return A tuple with the first element being a boolean indicating if the email is valid and the second element being the pattern used to check the email.
*/
std::tuple<bool, std::string> check_email(const std::string& email) {
    // A comprehensive regex for email validation.
    // This pattern aims to cover most valid email formats according to RFC 5322,
    // while also being practical for common use cases.
    //
    // Breakdown:
    // ^                                      - Start of the string
    // [a-zA-Z0-9._%+-]+                      - Local part: allows alphanumeric, ., _, %, +, -
    //                                        - Note: This is a simplified local part. More complex rules exist (e.g., quoted strings),
    //                                        - but this covers the vast majority of common valid emails.
    // @                                      - The '@' symbol
    // [a-zA-Z0-9.-]+                         - Domain part: allows alphanumeric, ., -
    // \.                                     - A literal dot separating domain labels
    // [a-zA-Z]{2,}                           - Top-level domain (TLD): at least two alphabetic characters
    // (?:                                    - Non-capturing group for subdomains and multi-part TLDs
    //     \.                                 - A literal dot
    //     [a-zA-Z]{2,}                       - Another domain label or TLD part
    // )*                                     - Zero or more of these subdomains/TLD parts
    // $                                      - End of the string
    //
    // Refinements for common issues:
    // - Prevents consecutive dots in the local part: `[a-zA-Z0-9._%+-]+` implicitly handles this to some extent,
    //   but a more robust check would be `^[a-zA-Z0-9]+(?:[._%+-][a-zA-Z0-9]+)*@...`
    // - Prevents dots at the start/end of the local part: similar to above.
    // - Prevents hyphens at the start/end of domain labels: `[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+` allows this. A more strict pattern
    //   for domain labels would be `[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?`
    //
    // A more robust and commonly accepted regex for practical purposes:
    const std::string email_pattern =
        R"(^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\])|(([a-zA-Z\-0-9]+\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}))$)";

    std::regex email_regex(email_pattern);
    bool is_valid = std::regex_match(email, email_regex);

    return std::make_tuple(is_valid, email_pattern);
}
```
